(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("service-mesh",{"service-mesh/kubernetes-networking-under-the-hood":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes promises that every pod can reach every other pod by IP address, without NAT. Something has to make that true. Knowing what that something is turns a mysterious timeout into a short list of places to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The four rules of the Kubernetes network model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a CNI plugin does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a Service, kube-proxy and endpoints turn a name into a pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How DNS works inside a cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingress and the Gateway API for traffic coming from outside\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model\">The model\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Every pod gets its own IP address.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pods can reach all other pods without NAT.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Agents on a node can reach all pods on that node.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A pod sees itself with the same IP address others see.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>The cluster does not implement this itself. A \u003cstrong>CNI plugin\u003c/strong> (Container Network Interface), such as Calico, Cilium or Flannel, runs when a pod starts. It creates a virtual network device for the pod, assigns an address from the node&#39;s pod range and sets up the routes or tunnels that let other nodes reach it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-a-service-name-to-a-pod\">From a Service name to a pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pods come and go, so their addresses change. A \u003cstrong>Service\u003c/strong> gives a stable virtual IP (the ClusterIP) and a name. The pieces:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Service has a \u003cstrong>selector\u003c/strong>. Pods whose labels match become its \u003cstrong>endpoints\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>kube-proxy\u003c/strong> on each node programs rules (iptables or IPVS) that rewrite traffic sent to the ClusterIP so it goes to one of the endpoint pod IPs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CoreDNS\u003c/strong> answers \u003ccode>web.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code> with the ClusterIP.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get svc,endpoints web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc,endpoints web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME          TYPE        CLUSTER-IP     PORT(S)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">service/web   ClusterIP   10.96.14.20    80/TCP\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            ENDPOINTS                         AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">endpoints/web   10.244.1.7:8080,10.244.2.4:8080   3d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the \u003cstrong>ENDPOINTS\u003c/strong> column is empty, the selector matches no ready pods. That is the most common cause of &quot;the Service does not work&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dns-in-the-cluster\">DNS in the cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pods get \u003ccode>/etc/resolv.conf\u003c/code> pointing at CoreDNS and a search path such as \u003ccode>shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local\u003c/code>. So \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> resolves inside the \u003ccode>shop\u003c/code> namespace, and \u003ccode>web.other\u003c/code> reaches another namespace. A setting called \u003ccode>ndots:5\u003c/code> makes short names try the search path first, which can slow lookups for external names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coming-in-from-outside\">Coming in from outside\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>NodePort\u003c/strong> opens a port on every node. A \u003cstrong>LoadBalancer\u003c/strong> Service asks the cloud (or MetalLB on bare metal) for an external IP.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ingress\u003c/strong> routes HTTP by host and path to Services, run by an ingress controller such as NGINX.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>Gateway API\u003c/strong> is the newer, more expressive model: a \u003ccode>Gateway\u003c/code> for the listener, \u003ccode>HTTPRoute\u003c/code> for rules, with roles split between platform and application teams.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Clients get &quot;connection refused&quot; through a Service called web. Fix it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># restart the pods and the Service\nkubectl rollout restart deployment/web -n shop\nkubectl delete svc web -n shop &amp;&amp; kubectl apply -f svc.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl get endpoints web -n shop\nNAME   ENDPOINTS   AGE\nweb    &lt;none&gt;      12s\n# still no endpoints, and now there was an outage while the Service was deleted\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Restarting changes nothing about labels. The Service selects app=web but the pods are labelled app=webapp, so no pod matches. Deleting the Service also removed its ClusterIP for a while.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl get svc web -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'\nkubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels\n# fix the selector or the label so they match\nkubectl get endpoints web -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Compare the selector with the pod labels first. A Service with empty endpoints is a label problem until proven otherwise.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Kubernetes networking under the hood fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">From a Service name to a pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">DNS in the cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Coming in from outside\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The model to From a Service name to a pod; 2, From a Service name to a pod to DNS in the cluster; 3, DNS in the cluster to Coming in from outside.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The model \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every pod gets its own IP address.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blaming the app when the Service has no endpoints.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>From a Service name to a pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pods come and go, so their addresses change.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting that the selector and labels must match exactly.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>DNS in the cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pods get /etc/resolv.conf pointing at CoreDNS and a search path such as shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming DNS is always fast inside a cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Coming in from outside \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A NodePort opens a port on every node. A LoadBalancer Service asks the cloud (or MetalLB on bare metal) for an external IP.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using NodePort in production without a load balancer in front.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-dns-slowed-everything-down\">Incident: DNS slowed everything down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Calls to an external API took five seconds, but only from pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-7d -- cat /etc/resolv.conf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-7d -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/resolv.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nameserver 10.96.0.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">options ndots:5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The name \u003ccode>api.partner.com\u003c/code> has two dots, fewer than five, so the resolver tried each search suffix first and waited for failures. Ending the name with a dot (\u003ccode>api.partner.com.\u003c/code>) or lowering \u003ccode>ndots\u003c/code> cut the time to milliseconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Blaming the app when the Service has no endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that the selector and labels must match exactly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming DNS is always fast inside a cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using NodePort in production without a load balancer in front\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model","text":"The model"},{"id":"from-a-service-name-to-a-pod","text":"From a Service name to a pod"},{"id":"dns-in-the-cluster","text":"DNS in the cluster"},{"id":"coming-in-from-outside","text":"Coming in from outside"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-dns-slowed-everything-down","text":"Incident: DNS slowed everything down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Cluster networking","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/networking/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Service","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/"},{"t":"Gateway API","u":"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"}],"text":"Kubernetes promises that every pod can reach every other pod by IP address, without NAT. Something has to make that true. Knowing what that something is turns a mysterious timeout into a short list of places to look. What you will learn The four rules of the Kubernetes network model What a CNI plugin does How a Service, kube-proxy and endpoints turn a name into a pod How DNS works inside a cluster Ingress and the Gateway API for traffic coming from outside The model Every pod gets its own IP address. Pods can reach all other pods without NAT. Agents on a node can reach all pods on that node. A pod sees itself with the same IP address others see. The cluster does not implement this itself. A CNI plugin (Container Network Interface), such as Calico, Cilium or Flannel, runs when a pod starts. It creates a virtual network device for the pod, assigns an address from the node&#39;s pod range and sets up the routes or tunnels that let other nodes reach it. From a Service name to a pod Pods come and go, so their addresses change. A Service gives a stable virtual IP (the ClusterIP) and a name. The pieces: The Service has a selector . Pods whose labels match become its endpoints . kube-proxy on each node programs rules (iptables or IPVS) that rewrite traffic sent to the ClusterIP so it goes to one of the endpoint pod IPs. CoreDNS answers web.shop.svc.cluster.local with the ClusterIP. $ kubectl get svc,endpoints web -n shop NAME TYPE CLUSTER-IP PORT(S) service/web ClusterIP 10.96.14.20 80/TCP NAME ENDPOINTS AGE endpoints/web 10.244.1.7:8080,10.244.2.4:8080 3d If the ENDPOINTS column is empty, the selector matches no ready pods. That is the most common cause of \"the Service does not work\". DNS in the cluster Pods get /etc/resolv.conf pointing at CoreDNS and a search path such as shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local . So web resolves inside the shop namespace, and web.other reaches another namespace. A setting called ndots:5 makes short names try the search path first, which can slow lookups for external names. Coming in from outside A NodePort opens a port on every node. A LoadBalancer Service asks the cloud (or MetalLB on bare metal) for an external IP. Ingress routes HTTP by host and path to Services, run by an ingress controller such as NGINX. The Gateway API is the newer, more expressive model: a Gateway for the listener, HTTPRoute for rules, with roles split between platform and application teams. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Clients get \"connection refused\" through a Service called web. Fix it. The tempting way # restart the pods and the Service kubectl rollout restart deployment/web -n shop kubectl delete svc web -n shop && kubectl apply -f svc.yaml See what goes wrong $ kubectl get endpoints web -n shop NAME ENDPOINTS AGE web \u003cnone> 12s # still no endpoints, and now there was an outage while the Service was deleted Why it fails. Restarting changes nothing about labels. The Service selects app=web but the pods are labelled app=webapp, so no pod matches. Deleting the Service also removed its ClusterIP for a while. See the way that works The way that works kubectl get svc web -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}' kubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels # fix the selector or the label so they match kubectl get endpoints web -n shop Compare the selector with the pod labels first. A Service with empty endpoints is a label problem until proven otherwise. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Kubernetes networking under the hood fits together Flow in order: 1, The model to From a Service name to a pod; 2, From a Service name to a pod to DNS in the cluster; 3, DNS in the cluster to Coming in from outside. Step Network edge Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The model Step "},"service-mesh/cni-network-policy-and-ebpf":{"html":"\u003cp>By default every pod can talk to every other pod. That is convenient for a small cluster and risky for a shared one. Network policies and the right CNI let you say who may talk to whom.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How CNIs differ: overlay, routed, eBPF\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a NetworkPolicy does and the default-deny pattern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why a policy does nothing without support from the CNI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What eBPF changes in Cilium\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to test a policy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-a-cni\">Choosing a CNI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Flannel\u003c/strong>: simple overlay networking, no policy support.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Calico\u003c/strong>: routed or overlay networking with strong policy and BGP options.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cilium\u003c/strong>: built on \u003cstrong>eBPF\u003c/strong>, with policy, observability and optional replacement of kube-proxy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) give pods addresses from the cloud network.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>overlay\u003c/strong> wraps pod traffic in tunnels (VXLAN) between nodes, which is easy but adds a small overhead. \u003cstrong>Routed\u003c/strong> designs advertise pod ranges to the network, which is faster and easier to debug with ordinary tools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-networkpolicy-says\">What a NetworkPolicy says\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists the traffic \u003cstrong>allowed\u003c/strong> to or from them. Policies are additive: if no policy selects a pod, everything is allowed; once any policy selects it for a direction, only what is listed in some policy is allowed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]\n---\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: web-from-ingress\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: web\n  policyTypes: [Ingress]\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - namespaceSelector:\n        matchLabels:\n          name: ingress-nginx\n    ports:\n    - port: 8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Egress\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web-from-ingress\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaceSelector:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ingress-nginx\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first policy selects every pod and allows nothing: \u003cstrong>default deny\u003c/strong>. The second allows only the ingress controller to reach \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> on 8080. Remember to allow DNS (port 53 to CoreDNS) when you deny egress, or lookups break.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"policy-needs-enforcement\">Policy needs enforcement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy is only a rule. The \u003cstrong>CNI\u003c/strong> has to enforce it. Applying one on a cluster running Flannel is accepted without error and does nothing, which is a dangerous silent failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-ebpf-changes\">What eBPF changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>eBPF\u003c/strong> lets small, checked programs run inside the Linux kernel. Cilium uses it to:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enforce policy by identity (labels), not by IP address\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replace long iptables chains with fast hash lookups, which matters with thousands of Services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Show who talks to whom, with tools like Hubble\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cilium status --brief\nkubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- curl -s -m 2 http://db.shop:5432\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cilium status --brief\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OK\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> -n shop deploy/web -- curl -s -m 2 http://db.shop:5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (28) Connection timed out after 2001 milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A timeout where you expect a refusal often means a policy is silently dropping the packets.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Lock down the shop namespace so only the ingress controller can reach the web pods.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># apply a default-deny policy and nothing else, in production, on a Friday\nkubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- nslookup db.shop\n;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\n# every pod lost DNS and all traffic stopped, including health checks\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Default deny with Egress included blocks DNS and every other call. Nothing was allowed first, so the whole namespace went dark at once.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># 1. observe real traffic first (Hubble or flow logs)\n# 2. write allow rules: ingress to web, web to db, all pods to kube-dns on 53\n# 3. test in a staging namespace\n# 4. apply default-deny last\nkubectl apply -f allow-dns.yaml -f allow-web.yaml -n shop\nkubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Add the allows before the deny, test them, and keep a rollback command ready.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How CNI, network policy and eBPF fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Choosing a CNI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What a NetworkPolicy says\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy needs enforcement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What eBPF changes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Choosing a CNI to What a NetworkPolicy says; 2, What a NetworkPolicy says to Policy needs enforcement; 3, Policy needs enforcement to What eBPF changes.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Choosing a CNI \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Flannel: simple overlay networking, no policy support.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using IP addresses in policy instead of labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>What a NetworkPolicy says \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists the traffic allowed to or from them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applying default-deny without first learning the real traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Policy needs enforcement \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A NetworkPolicy is only a rule. The CNI has to enforce it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming a policy is enforced without testing it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>What eBPF changes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> eBPF lets small, checked programs run inside the Linux kernel.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Denying egress without allowing DNS.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-policy-that-did-nothing\">Incident: The policy that did nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security review found the database reachable from any namespace, despite a NetworkPolicy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get cm -n kube-system | grep -E &quot;flannel|calico|cilium&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cm -n kube-system | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;flannel|calico|cilium&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kube-flannel-cfg   2   200d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster used plain Flannel, which does not enforce NetworkPolicy. The policy objects existed, but nothing applied them. The team moved to a CNI with enforcement and added a test that tries a blocked connection in CI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming a policy is enforced without testing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Denying egress without allowing DNS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using IP addresses in policy instead of labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying default-deny without first learning the real traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"choosing-a-cni","text":"Choosing a CNI"},{"id":"what-a-networkpolicy-says","text":"What a NetworkPolicy says"},{"id":"policy-needs-enforcement","text":"Policy needs enforcement"},{"id":"what-ebpf-changes","text":"What eBPF changes"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-policy-that-did-nothing","text":"Incident: The policy that did nothing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Network Policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"},{"t":"Cilium documentation","u":"https://docs.cilium.io/"},{"t":"Project Calico documentation","u":"https://docs.tigera.io/calico/latest/about/"}],"text":"By default every pod can talk to every other pod. That is convenient for a small cluster and risky for a shared one. Network policies and the right CNI let you say who may talk to whom. What you will learn How CNIs differ: overlay, routed, eBPF What a NetworkPolicy does and the default-deny pattern Why a policy does nothing without support from the CNI What eBPF changes in Cilium How to test a policy Choosing a CNI Flannel : simple overlay networking, no policy support. Calico : routed or overlay networking with strong policy and BGP options. Cilium : built on eBPF , with policy, observability and optional replacement of kube-proxy. Cloud CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) give pods addresses from the cloud network. An overlay wraps pod traffic in tunnels (VXLAN) between nodes, which is easy but adds a small overhead. Routed designs advertise pod ranges to the network, which is faster and easier to debug with ordinary tools. What a NetworkPolicy says A NetworkPolicy selects pods and lists the traffic allowed to or from them. Policies are additive: if no policy selects a pod, everything is allowed; once any policy selects it for a direction, only what is listed in some policy is allowed. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ Ingress , Egress ] --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: web-from-ingress namespace: shop spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: web policyTypes: [ Ingress ] ingress: - from: - namespaceSelector: matchLabels: name: ingress-nginx ports: - port: 8080 The first policy selects every pod and allows nothing: default deny . The second allows only the ingress controller to reach web on 8080. Remember to allow DNS (port 53 to CoreDNS) when you deny egress, or lookups break. Policy needs enforcement A NetworkPolicy is only a rule. The CNI has to enforce it. Applying one on a cluster running Flannel is accepted without error and does nothing, which is a dangerous silent failure. What eBPF changes eBPF lets small, checked programs run inside the Linux kernel. Cilium uses it to: Enforce policy by identity (labels), not by IP address Replace long iptables chains with fast hash lookups, which matters with thousands of Services Show who talks to whom, with tools like Hubble $ cilium status --brief OK $ kubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- curl -s -m 2 http://db.shop:5432 curl: (28) Connection timed out after 2001 milliseconds A timeout where you expect a refusal often means a policy is silently dropping the packets. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Lock down the shop namespace so only the ingress controller can reach the web pods. The tempting way # apply a default-deny policy and nothing else, in production, on a Friday kubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop See what goes wrong $ kubectl exec -n shop deploy/web -- nslookup db.shop ;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached # every pod lost DNS and all traffic stopped, including health checks Why it fails. Default deny with Egress included blocks DNS and every other call. Nothing was allowed first, so the whole namespace went dark at once. See the way that works The way that works # 1. observe real traffic first (Hubble or flow logs) # 2. write allow rules: ingress to web, web to db, all pods to kube-dns on 53 # 3. test in a staging namespace # 4. apply default-deny last kubectl apply -f allow-dns.yaml -f allow-web.yaml -n shop kubectl apply -f default-deny.yaml -n shop Add the allows before the deny, test them, and keep a rollback command ready. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How CNI, network policy and eBPF fits together Flow in order: 1, Choosing a CNI to What a NetworkPolicy says; 2, What a NetworkPolicy says to Policy needs enforcement; 3, Policy needs enforcemen"},"service-mesh/what-a-service-mesh-is-and-how-it-works":{"html":"\u003cp>When a company has hundreds of services, every one needs the same things: encrypted calls, retries, timeouts, metrics, access rules. A service mesh moves those jobs out of the application code and into the platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The problem a mesh solves, and what it costs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data plane and control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sidecar proxies, and the newer ambient mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The main meshes: Istio, Linkerd, Cilium\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to decide whether you need one\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-problem\">The problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Without a mesh, each team writes its own retry logic, its own TLS setup and its own metrics. They differ by language, and a fix means redeploying every service. With a mesh, a proxy beside each service handles the network side the same way for everyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-planes\">Two planes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>data plane\u003c/strong> is the proxies that carry the real traffic. In Istio the proxy is \u003cstrong>Envoy\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>control plane\u003c/strong> (Istio&#39;s \u003ccode>istiod\u003c/code>) is the brain. It watches Kubernetes and your mesh configuration, turns them into proxy configuration and pushes it out. It also issues the certificates used for mTLS.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop\nkubectl get pod web-7d9f-x2 -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7d9f-x2   2/2     Running   0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod web-7d9f-x2 -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[*].name}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web istio-proxy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>2/2\u003c/code> ready means the pod runs the app and a proxy sidecar. Traffic in and out of the app is redirected through the proxy automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sidecar-and-ambient\">Sidecar and ambient\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sidecar\u003c/strong>: a proxy in every pod. It is simple to reason about and gives per-pod control, but it costs memory and CPU in every pod, and pods must be restarted to pick up proxy upgrades.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ambient\u003c/strong> (Istio): a shared node-level component handles encryption and basic policy (L4), and an optional per-namespace proxy handles HTTP features (L7). It lowers the per-pod cost.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Injection works through a label on the namespace (\u003ccode>istio-injection=enabled\u003c/code>). Pods created \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> the label do not get a sidecar until they restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-main-meshes\">The main meshes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mesh\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Style\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Known for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Istio\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Envoy, rich features\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Powerful traffic control, larger to operate\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Linkerd\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Small Rust proxy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simplicity, low overhead\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cilium\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>eBPF, sidecar-free option\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Network integration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-not-to-use-one\">When not to use one\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A mesh adds moving parts, a new failure mode and a learning curve. If you have a handful of services, or need only TLS, a library or an ingress may be enough. Adopt a mesh for a clear need: mutual TLS everywhere, uniform telemetry, or fine traffic control.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Turn on the mesh for the shop namespace and get mTLS for all services.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># label the namespace and expect everything to change\nkubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl get pods -n shop\nNAME          READY   STATUS\nweb-7d9f-x2   1/1     Running\n# still one container: no sidecar, no mTLS\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Injection happens when a pod is created. Pods that already existed were never changed. The label only affects new pods.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled\nkubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop\nkubectl get pods -n shop\nistioctl proxy-status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Restart the workloads so the webhook can add the sidecar, then check that every pod reads 2/2 and every proxy shows SYNCED.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How What a service mesh is and how it works fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The problem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Two planes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sidecar and ambient\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The main meshes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When not to use one\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The problem to Two planes; 2, Two planes to Sidecar and ambient; 3, Sidecar and ambient to The main meshes; 4, The main meshes to When not to use one.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The problem \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Without a mesh, each team writes its own retry logic, its own TLS setup and its own metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adopting a mesh with no concrete problem to solve.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Two planes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The data plane is the proxies that carry the real traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Upgrading the control plane with no staged rollout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Sidecar and ambient \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sidecar: a proxy in every pod. It is simple to reason about and gives per-pod control, but it costs memory and CPU in every pod, and pods must be restarted to pick up...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expecting existing pods to get sidecars without a restart.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>The main meshes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> .\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>When not to use one \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A mesh adds moving parts, a new failure mode and a learning curve.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring the extra CPU and memory the proxies need.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-mesh-upgrade-that-took-the-checkout-down\">Incident: A mesh upgrade that took the checkout down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a control plane upgrade, some services returned 503 errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl proxy-status\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            CDS      LDS      EDS      RDS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout-1.shop STALE    SYNCED   SYNCED   SYNCED\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Some proxies were stuck on old configuration because the control plane restarted before they reconnected. Rolling the control plane one replica at a time, and waiting for proxy-status to show SYNCED before each next step, avoided the problem on the next upgrade.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adopting a mesh with no concrete problem to solve\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting existing pods to get sidecars without a restart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the extra CPU and memory the proxies need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading the control plane with no staged rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-problem","text":"The problem"},{"id":"two-planes","text":"Two planes"},{"id":"sidecar-and-ambient","text":"Sidecar and ambient"},{"id":"the-main-meshes","text":"The main meshes"},{"id":"when-not-to-use-one","text":"When not to use one"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-a-mesh-upgrade-that-took-the-checkout-down","text":"Incident: A mesh upgrade that took the checkout down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio: Architecture","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/deployment/architecture/"},{"t":"Linkerd documentation","u":"https://linkerd.io/2/overview/"},{"t":"Envoy documentation","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/"}],"text":"When a company has hundreds of services, every one needs the same things: encrypted calls, retries, timeouts, metrics, access rules. A service mesh moves those jobs out of the application code and into the platform. What you will learn The problem a mesh solves, and what it costs Data plane and control plane Sidecar proxies, and the newer ambient mode The main meshes: Istio, Linkerd, Cilium How to decide whether you need one The problem Without a mesh, each team writes its own retry logic, its own TLS setup and its own metrics. They differ by language, and a fix means redeploying every service. With a mesh, a proxy beside each service handles the network side the same way for everyone. Two planes The data plane is the proxies that carry the real traffic. In Istio the proxy is Envoy . The control plane (Istio&#39;s istiod ) is the brain. It watches Kubernetes and your mesh configuration, turns them into proxy configuration and pushes it out. It also issues the certificates used for mTLS. $ kubectl get pods -n shop NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS web-7d9f-x2 2/2 Running 0 $ kubectl get pod web-7d9f-x2 -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.containers[*].name}&#x27; web istio-proxy 2/2 ready means the pod runs the app and a proxy sidecar. Traffic in and out of the app is redirected through the proxy automatically. Sidecar and ambient Sidecar : a proxy in every pod. It is simple to reason about and gives per-pod control, but it costs memory and CPU in every pod, and pods must be restarted to pick up proxy upgrades. Ambient (Istio): a shared node-level component handles encryption and basic policy (L4), and an optional per-namespace proxy handles HTTP features (L7). It lowers the per-pod cost. Injection works through a label on the namespace ( istio-injection=enabled ). Pods created before the label do not get a sidecar until they restart. The main meshes Mesh Style Known for Istio Envoy, rich features Powerful traffic control, larger to operate Linkerd Small Rust proxy Simplicity, low overhead Cilium eBPF, sidecar-free option Network integration When not to use one A mesh adds moving parts, a new failure mode and a learning curve. If you have a handful of services, or need only TLS, a library or an ingress may be enough. Adopt a mesh for a clear need: mutual TLS everywhere, uniform telemetry, or fine traffic control. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Turn on the mesh for the shop namespace and get mTLS for all services. The tempting way # label the namespace and expect everything to change kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled See what goes wrong $ kubectl get pods -n shop NAME READY STATUS web-7d9f-x2 1/1 Running # still one container: no sidecar, no mTLS Why it fails. Injection happens when a pod is created. Pods that already existed were never changed. The label only affects new pods. See the way that works The way that works kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled kubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop kubectl get pods -n shop istioctl proxy-status Restart the workloads so the webhook can add the sidecar, then check that every pod reads 2/2 and every proxy shows SYNCED. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How What a service mesh is and how it works fits together Flow in order: 1, The problem to Two planes; 2, Two planes to Sidecar and ambient; 3, Sidecar and ambient to The main meshes; 4, The main meshes to When not to use one. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The problem Step What it does: Without a mesh, each team writes its own retry logic, its own TLS setup and its own metrics. If it fails or is weak: Adopting a mesh with no concrete problem to solve. Two planes Step What it does: The data plane is the proxies that carry the real traffic. If it fails o"},"service-mesh/mtls-and-traffic-management":{"html":"\u003cp>Once the proxies are in place, the mesh gives you two big powers: every call can be encrypted and authenticated, and you can steer traffic with configuration instead of code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mutual TLS, identity and the PeerAuthentication modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Authorisation policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VirtualService and DestinationRule for routing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary releases with weights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking, and how they can hurt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mutual-tls\">Mutual TLS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With ordinary TLS only the server proves who it is. In \u003cstrong>mutual TLS (mTLS)\u003c/strong> both sides do. The mesh gives each workload an identity (based on its service account), a short-lived certificate, and rotates it automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PeerAuthentication\u003c/code> sets the mode:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>PERMISSIVE\u003c/strong>: accepts both mTLS and plain text. Good while you migrate.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>STRICT\u003c/strong>: only mTLS. Plain text callers are rejected.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: PeerAuthentication\nmetadata:\n  name: default\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  mtls:\n    mode: STRICT\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">security.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PeerAuthentication\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mtls:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">STRICT\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003ccode>AuthorizationPolicy\u003c/code> then says who may call what, for example only the \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> service account may call \u003ccode>GET /orders\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routing-virtualservice-and-destinationrule\">Routing: VirtualService and DestinationRule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>DestinationRule\u003c/strong> defines \u003cstrong>subsets\u003c/strong> of a service, usually by pod label (\u003ccode>version: v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>version: v2\u003c/code>), and policies such as connection limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>VirtualService\u003c/strong> decides where requests go: by weight, by header, by path.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: DestinationRule\nmetadata:\n  name: reviews\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  host: reviews\n  subsets:\n  - name: v1\n    labels:\n      version: v1\n  - name: v2\n    labels:\n      version: v2\n---\napiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: VirtualService\nmetadata:\n  name: reviews\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  hosts:\n  - reviews\n  http:\n  - route:\n    - destination:\n        host: reviews\n        subset: v1\n      weight: 90\n    - destination:\n        host: reviews\n        subset: v2\n      weight: 10\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DestinationRule\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subsets:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualService\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This sends 10 percent of calls to version 2: a \u003cstrong>canary\u003c/strong>. Raise the weight as metrics stay healthy. The weights in one route must add up to 100, and a subset named in a VirtualService must exist in a DestinationRule, or the proxy returns errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl analyze -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl analyze -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: &quot;reviews+v2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-retries-and-circuit-breaking\">Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>timeout\u003c/strong> stops a call from waiting forever.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>retry\u003c/strong> repeats a failed call, which hides short faults but can multiply load.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Circuit breaking\u003c/strong> (connection limits and outlier detection) stops sending traffic to a pod that keeps failing.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>If service A retries three times and calls B, which retries three times and calls C, one slow C produces up to nine calls for each request: a \u003cstrong>retry storm\u003c/strong>. Put retries at one layer, use short per-try timeouts and cap the total.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Send 10 percent of reviews traffic to version 2 as a canary.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># apply only the VirtualService\nkubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ curl -s http://reviews:9080/ -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code}\n&quot;\n503\n$ istioctl analyze -n shop\nError [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: &quot;reviews+v2&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> A VirtualService can name a subset, but the subset is defined in a DestinationRule. Without it the proxy has no endpoints for &quot;v2&quot; and answers 503 to the requests routed there.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl apply -f reviews-destinationrule.yaml\nkubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml\nistioctl analyze -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Create the DestinationRule first, then the VirtualService, and run analyze before you watch the dashboards.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How mTLS and traffic management fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mutual TLS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Routing: VirtualService and\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Timeouts, retries and circuit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Mutual TLS to Routing: VirtualService and; 2, Routing: VirtualService and to Timeouts, retries and circuit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Mutual TLS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> With ordinary TLS only the server proves who it is.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Switching to STRICT before every caller has a sidecar.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Routing: VirtualService and \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A DestinationRule defines subsets of a service, usually by pod label (version: v1, version: v2), and policies such as connection limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting that a VirtualService needs its subsets to exist.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Timeouts, retries and circuit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A timeout stops a call from waiting forever.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Weights that do not add up to 100.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-retries-made-the-outage-worse\">Incident: Retries made the outage worse\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A slow database caused a full outage of the storefront.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Three layers each retried failed calls three times with no limit. When the database slowed, each user request became dozens of calls, which overloaded it further. Removing retries from the inner layers, adding per-try timeouts, and enabling outlier detection restored stability.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Switching to STRICT before every caller has a sidecar\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Weights that do not add up to 100\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries at several layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a VirtualService needs its subsets to exist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"mutual-tls","text":"Mutual TLS"},{"id":"routing-virtualservice-and-destinationrule","text":"Routing: VirtualService and DestinationRule"},{"id":"timeouts-retries-and-circuit-breaking","text":"Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-retries-made-the-outage-worse","text":"Incident: Retries made the outage worse"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio: Traffic management","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/traffic-management/"},{"t":"Istio: Security","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"Envoy: Circuit breaking","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/upstream/circuit_breaking"}],"text":"Once the proxies are in place, the mesh gives you two big powers: every call can be encrypted and authenticated, and you can steer traffic with configuration instead of code. What you will learn Mutual TLS, identity and the PeerAuthentication modes Authorisation policy VirtualService and DestinationRule for routing Canary releases with weights Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking, and how they can hurt Mutual TLS With ordinary TLS only the server proves who it is. In mutual TLS (mTLS) both sides do. The mesh gives each workload an identity (based on its service account), a short-lived certificate, and rotates it automatically. PeerAuthentication sets the mode: PERMISSIVE : accepts both mTLS and plain text. Good while you migrate. STRICT : only mTLS. Plain text callers are rejected. apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1 kind: PeerAuthentication metadata: name: default namespace: shop spec: mtls: mode: STRICT An AuthorizationPolicy then says who may call what, for example only the web service account may call GET /orders . Routing: VirtualService and DestinationRule A DestinationRule defines subsets of a service, usually by pod label ( version: v1 , version: v2 ), and policies such as connection limits. A VirtualService decides where requests go: by weight, by header, by path. apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: DestinationRule metadata: name: reviews namespace: shop spec: host: reviews subsets: - name: v1 labels: version: v1 - name: v2 labels: version: v2 --- apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: VirtualService metadata: name: reviews namespace: shop spec: hosts: - reviews http: - route: - destination: host: reviews subset: v1 weight: 90 - destination: host: reviews subset: v2 weight: 10 This sends 10 percent of calls to version 2: a canary . Raise the weight as metrics stay healthy. The weights in one route must add up to 100, and a subset named in a VirtualService must exist in a DestinationRule, or the proxy returns errors. $ istioctl analyze -n shop Error [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: \"reviews+v2\" Timeouts, retries and circuit breaking A timeout stops a call from waiting forever. A retry repeats a failed call, which hides short faults but can multiply load. Circuit breaking (connection limits and outlier detection) stops sending traffic to a pod that keeps failing. If service A retries three times and calls B, which retries three times and calls C, one slow C produces up to nine calls for each request: a retry storm . Put retries at one layer, use short per-try timeouts and cap the total. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Send 10 percent of reviews traffic to version 2 as a canary. The tempting way # apply only the VirtualService kubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml See what goes wrong $ curl -s http://reviews:9080/ -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} \" 503 $ istioctl analyze -n shop Error [IST0101] (VirtualService shop/reviews) Referenced host+subset in destinationrule not found: \"reviews+v2\" Why it fails. A VirtualService can name a subset, but the subset is defined in a DestinationRule. Without it the proxy has no endpoints for \"v2\" and answers 503 to the requests routed there. See the way that works The way that works kubectl apply -f reviews-destinationrule.yaml kubectl apply -f reviews-virtualservice.yaml istioctl analyze -n shop Create the DestinationRule first, then the VirtualService, and run analyze before you watch the dashboards. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How mTLS and traffic management fits together Flow in order: 1, Mutual TLS to Routing: VirtualService and; 2, Routing: VirtualService and to Timeouts, retries and circuit. Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Mutual TLS Security"},"service-mesh/observing-and-troubleshooting-the-mesh":{"html":"\u003cp>A mesh adds a hop to every call, which makes problems harder to place: is it the app, the proxy or the configuration? The same proxies that add the hop also give you the best visibility you will ever have.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The metrics and traces a mesh produces for free\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking sync with \u003ccode>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>analyze\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading proxy response flags in access logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A step-by-step method for a failing call\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-get-for-free\">What you get for free\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every proxy reports the \u003cstrong>golden signals\u003c/strong> for each service pair: request rate, error rate, latency and saturation. Add distributed tracing headers and you can see a request&#39;s path through services. Dashboards such as Kiali (a topology graph), Grafana and Jaeger show it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"is-the-configuration-there\">Is the configuration there?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before blaming the app, check that the proxy has the configuration you wrote.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl proxy-status\nistioctl analyze -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME             CLUSTER      CDS      LDS      EDS      RDS      ISTIOD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7d.shop      Kubernetes   SYNCED   SYNCED   SYNCED   SYNCED   istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">reviews-9a.shop  Kubernetes   SYNCED   STALE    SYNCED   SYNCED   istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl analyze -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Info [IST0118] (Service shop/web) Port name  (port: 80, targetPort: 8080) doesn't follow the naming convention of Istio port.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>STALE\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>NOT SENT\u003c/strong> means the proxy has not received or accepted the latest configuration.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>analyze\u003c/code> checks your configuration for mistakes. Port names that do not start with a protocol (\u003ccode>http-\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>grpc-\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tcp-\u003c/code>) make the mesh treat the traffic as plain TCP, so HTTP features silently do not apply.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-response-flags\">Reading response flags\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Envoy access logs show a short \u003cstrong>response flag\u003c/strong> that says why a request failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Flag\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Look at\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>UF\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Upstream connection failure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Is the destination pod up? mTLS mode mismatch?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>UC\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Upstream connection terminated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>App closed the connection, timeouts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>NR\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No route configured\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VirtualService host or Gateway\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>UH\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No healthy upstream\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Endpoints empty, readiness probes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>URX\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retry limit exceeded\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backend failing; retries too high\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DC\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Downstream connection terminated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Client gave up\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[2026-11-03T10:12:01.221Z] &quot;GET /orders HTTP/1.1&quot; 503 UH no_healthy_upstream - 0 19 0 - &quot;-&quot; &quot;curl/8&quot; &quot;reviews.shop.svc.cluster.local:9080&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>503 UH\u003c/code> for the reviews service tells you no healthy endpoint exists, so check the selector, readiness and rollout of reviews.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-method-for-a-failing-call\">A method for a failing call\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Which hop?\u003c/strong> Use the trace or the access logs to find the first failing hop.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Does the proxy have current config?\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Is the config valid?\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>istioctl analyze\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What does the flag say?\u003c/strong> Read the response flag in the log.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Does the destination have endpoints?\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>kubectl get endpoints\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Is it a policy?\u003c/strong> Check PeerAuthentication and AuthorizationPolicy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Take the mesh out of the picture.\u003c/strong> Call the pod IP directly to see whether the app is the problem.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Calls from web to reviews suddenly return 503. Find the cause.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># assume the app is broken and redeploy reviews, then scale it up\nkubectl rollout restart deployment/reviews -n shop\nkubectl scale deployment/reviews --replicas=10 -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -1\n&quot;GET /reviews HTTP/1.1&quot; 503 UF upstream_reset_before_response_started{connection_termination}\n# still failing with more pods\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> UF means the connection to the upstream failed. The new PeerAuthentication in reviews is STRICT, but web has no sidecar so it calls in plain text. More replicas cannot fix a protocol mismatch.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kubectl get pods -n shop\nistioctl proxy-status\nkubectl get peerauthentication -A\n# give web a sidecar, or set PERMISSIVE until it has one\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Read the response flag before changing anything. UF with a recent mTLS change is a mismatch between a plain text caller and a STRICT destination.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Observing and troubleshooting the mesh fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What you get for free\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Is the configuration there?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reading response flags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A method for a failing call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What you get for free to Is the configuration there?; 2, Is the configuration there? to Reading response flags; 3, Reading response flags to A method for a failing call.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What you get for free \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every proxy reports the golden signals for each service pair: request rate, error rate, latency and saturation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Looking at the app first when the proxy configuration is stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Is the configuration there? \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Before blaming the app, check that the proxy has the configuration you wrote.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Port names without a protocol prefix.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Reading response flags \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Envoy access logs show a short response flag that says why a request failed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring response flags.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>A method for a failing call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Which hop?. Use the trace or the access logs to find the first failing hop.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everything-is-up-nothing-works\">Incident: Everything is up, nothing works\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The checkout page returned errors, but every pod was Running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>istioctl proxy-status\u003c/code> showed 40 proxies STALE. The control plane had run out of memory and restarted, and it could not push new configuration. Raising its memory limit and adding a second replica fixed it. Without proxy-status the team would have spent hours inspecting healthy pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking at the app first when the proxy configuration is stale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Port names without a protocol prefix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring response flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a single control plane replica\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-you-get-for-free","text":"What you get for free"},{"id":"is-the-configuration-there","text":"Is the configuration there?"},{"id":"reading-response-flags","text":"Reading response flags"},{"id":"a-method-for-a-failing-call","text":"A method for a failing call"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-everything-is-up-nothing-works","text":"Incident: Everything is up, nothing works"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio: Debugging","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/diagnostic-tools/"},{"t":"Envoy: Access logging response flags","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/configuration/observability/access_log/usage"},{"t":"Google SRE book: Monitoring","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"}],"text":"A mesh adds a hop to every call, which makes problems harder to place: is it the app, the proxy or the configuration? The same proxies that add the hop also give you the best visibility you will ever have. What you will learn The metrics and traces a mesh produces for free Checking sync with istioctl proxy-status and analyze Reading proxy response flags in access logs A step-by-step method for a failing call What you get for free Every proxy reports the golden signals for each service pair: request rate, error rate, latency and saturation. Add distributed tracing headers and you can see a request&#39;s path through services. Dashboards such as Kiali (a topology graph), Grafana and Jaeger show it. Is the configuration there? Before blaming the app, check that the proxy has the configuration you wrote. $ istioctl proxy-status NAME CLUSTER CDS LDS EDS RDS ISTIOD web-7d.shop Kubernetes SYNCED SYNCED SYNCED SYNCED istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp reviews-9a.shop Kubernetes SYNCED STALE SYNCED SYNCED istiod-6d4b9c7f5-xk2lp $ istioctl analyze -n shop Info [IST0118] (Service shop/web) Port name (port: 80, targetPort: 8080) doesn't follow the naming convention of Istio port. STALE or NOT SENT means the proxy has not received or accepted the latest configuration. analyze checks your configuration for mistakes. Port names that do not start with a protocol ( http- , grpc- , tcp- ) make the mesh treat the traffic as plain TCP, so HTTP features silently do not apply. Reading response flags Envoy access logs show a short response flag that says why a request failed. Flag Meaning Look at UF Upstream connection failure Is the destination pod up? mTLS mode mismatch? UC Upstream connection terminated App closed the connection, timeouts NR No route configured VirtualService host or Gateway UH No healthy upstream Endpoints empty, readiness probes URX Retry limit exceeded Backend failing; retries too high DC Downstream connection terminated Client gave up $ kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -2 [2026-11-03T10:12:01.221Z] \"GET /orders HTTP/1.1\" 503 UH no_healthy_upstream - 0 19 0 - \"-\" \"curl/8\" \"reviews.shop.svc.cluster.local:9080\" 503 UH for the reviews service tells you no healthy endpoint exists, so check the selector, readiness and rollout of reviews. A method for a failing call Which hop? Use the trace or the access logs to find the first failing hop. Does the proxy have current config? istioctl proxy-status . Is the config valid? istioctl analyze . What does the flag say? Read the response flag in the log. Does the destination have endpoints? kubectl get endpoints . Is it a policy? Check PeerAuthentication and AuthorizationPolicy. Take the mesh out of the picture. Call the pod IP directly to see whether the app is the problem. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Calls from web to reviews suddenly return 503. Find the cause. The tempting way # assume the app is broken and redeploy reviews, then scale it up kubectl rollout restart deployment/reviews -n shop kubectl scale deployment/reviews --replicas=10 -n shop See what goes wrong $ kubectl logs web-7d -c istio-proxy | tail -1 \"GET /reviews HTTP/1.1\" 503 UF upstream_reset_before_response_started{connection_termination} # still failing with more pods Why it fails. UF means the connection to the upstream failed. The new PeerAuthentication in reviews is STRICT, but web has no sidecar so it calls in plain text. More replicas cannot fix a protocol mismatch. See the way that works The way that works kubectl get pods -n shop istioctl proxy-status kubectl get peerauthentication -A # give web a sidecar, or set PERMISSIVE until it has one Read the response flag before changing anything. UF with a recent mTLS change is a mismatch between a plain text caller and a STRICT destination. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Observing and troublesho"},"service-mesh/sidecar-and-ambient-mesh-data-planes":{"html":"\u003cp>The data plane is the set of proxies that carry traffic. How they are deployed decides what the mesh costs and how risky it is to operate, so it is worth understanding both main designs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How sidecar injection works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How ambient mode splits layer 4 and layer 7\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overhead, upgrades and isolation trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a model for your cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sidecar-model\">Sidecar model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A mutating webhook adds a proxy container to each pod. Traffic in and out is redirected through it, so it can encrypt, retry, route and measure every call.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled\nkubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop\nkubectl get pod -n shop -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[*].name}'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled\nkubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop\nkubectl get pod -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.items[0].spec.containers[*].name}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cost is one proxy per pod: extra memory, CPU, start-up time and a rolling restart of every workload to upgrade proxies. The benefit is strong isolation, because each workload has its own proxy and policy point.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ambient-model\">Ambient model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In \u003cstrong>ambient mode\u003c/strong> a small per-node component handles encryption and basic layer 4 policy for all pods on that node. If you need layer 7 features such as header routing or retries, you add a \u003cstrong>waypoint\u003c/strong> proxy for a namespace or service.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label namespace shop istio.io/dataplane-mode=ambient\nistioctl waypoint apply -n shop --enroll-namespace\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl label namespace shop istio.io/dataplane-mode=ambient\nistioctl waypoint apply -n shop --enroll-namespace\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Applications are not restarted to join, and the shared proxies are upgraded without touching pods. Trade-offs include a shared node component with a wider blast radius and fewer years of production experience than sidecars.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Measure the real overhead of proxies in a test cluster. Use sidecars for strict per-workload isolation and mature features. Consider ambient for many small pods or when restarting workloads is costly. Whichever you choose, keep a plan for upgrading the data plane safely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Sidecar and ambient mesh data planes fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sidecar model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ambient model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Choosing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Sidecar model to Ambient model; 2, Ambient model to Choosing.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Sidecar model \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A mutating webhook adds a proxy container to each pod.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting that proxy upgrades need pod restarts in the sidecar model.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Ambient model \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In ambient mode a small per-node component handles encryption and basic layer 4 policy for all pods on that node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using layer 7 features without a waypoint in ambient mode.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Choosing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Measure the real overhead of proxies in a test cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-memory-eaten-by-proxies\">Incident: Memory eaten by proxies\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Sidecar proxies used more memory than the applications they served.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kubectl top pod --containers -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app          38Mi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">istio-proxy  96Mi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4200 pods in the cluster\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Small services were wrapped with a default proxy that needed more memory than the service. The team tuned proxy limits for small workloads, scoped sidecar configuration to reduce memory use and moved low-risk namespaces to the shared node model after testing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enabling a mesh everywhere before measuring cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that proxy upgrades need pod restarts in the sidecar model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using layer 7 features without a waypoint in ambient mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the blast radius of a shared node component\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sidecar-model","text":"Sidecar model"},{"id":"ambient-model","text":"Ambient model"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-memory-eaten-by-proxies","text":"Incident: Memory eaten by proxies"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio documentation","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/"},{"t":"Linkerd documentation","u":"https://linkerd.io/2/overview/"}],"text":"The data plane is the set of proxies that carry traffic. How they are deployed decides what the mesh costs and how risky it is to operate, so it is worth understanding both main designs. What you will learn How sidecar injection works How ambient mode splits layer 4 and layer 7 Overhead, upgrades and isolation trade-offs Choosing a model for your cluster Sidecar model A mutating webhook adds a proxy container to each pod. Traffic in and out is redirected through it, so it can encrypt, retry, route and measure every call. kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled kubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop kubectl get pod -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.items[0].spec.containers[*].name}&#x27; The cost is one proxy per pod: extra memory, CPU, start-up time and a rolling restart of every workload to upgrade proxies. The benefit is strong isolation, because each workload has its own proxy and policy point. Ambient model In ambient mode a small per-node component handles encryption and basic layer 4 policy for all pods on that node. If you need layer 7 features such as header routing or retries, you add a waypoint proxy for a namespace or service. kubectl label namespace shop istio.io/dataplane-mode=ambient istioctl waypoint apply -n shop --enroll-namespace Applications are not restarted to join, and the shared proxies are upgraded without touching pods. Trade-offs include a shared node component with a wider blast radius and fewer years of production experience than sidecars. Choosing Measure the real overhead of proxies in a test cluster. Use sidecars for strict per-workload isolation and mature features. Consider ambient for many small pods or when restarting workloads is costly. Whichever you choose, keep a plan for upgrading the data plane safely. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Sidecar and ambient mesh data planes fits together Flow in order: 1, Sidecar model to Ambient model; 2, Ambient model to Choosing. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Sidecar model Step What it does: A mutating webhook adds a proxy container to each pod. If it fails or is weak: Forgetting that proxy upgrades need pod restarts in the sidecar model. Ambient model Step What it does: In ambient mode a small per-node component handles encryption and basic layer 4 policy for all pods on that node. If it fails or is weak: Using layer 7 features without a waypoint in ambient mode. Choosing Step What it does: Measure the real overhead of proxies in a test cluster. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: Memory eaten by proxies Incident Sidecar proxies used more memory than the applications they served. kubectl top pod --containers -n shop app 38Mi istio-proxy 96Mi 4200 pods in the cluster Small services were wrapped with a default proxy that needed more memory than the service. The team tuned proxy limits for small workloads, scoped sidecar configuration to reduce memory use and moved low-risk namespaces to the shared node model after testing. Common mistakes Enabling a mesh everywhere before measuring cost Forgetting that proxy upgrades need pod restarts in the sidecar model Using layer 7 features without a waypoint in ambient mode Ignoring the blast radius of a shared node component"},"service-mesh/ingress-gateways-and-the-gateway-api":{"html":"\u003cp>Traffic enters a cluster through a gateway before it meets the mesh. The older Ingress resource was limited, and the Gateway API replaces it with roles, richer routing and a standard that many implementations share.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The roles of GatewayClass, Gateway and routes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Routing by host, path and header\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>TLS termination and certificate handling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Timeouts, limits and safe change control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resources\">Resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Gateway\nmetadata:\n  name: public\n  namespace: infra\nspec:\n  gatewayClassName: istio\n  listeners:\n    - name: https\n      protocol: HTTPS\n      port: 443\n      hostname: shop.example.com\n      tls:\n        mode: Terminate\n        certificateRefs:\n          - name: shop-cert\n      allowedRoutes:\n        namespaces:\n          from: Selector\n          selector:\n            matchLabels:\n              expose: &quot;true&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Gateway\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">public\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">infra\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">gatewayClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">istio\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">listeners:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">protocol:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">HTTPS\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">443\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostname:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tls:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Terminate\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">certificateRefs:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-cert\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowedRoutes:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaces:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Selector\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expose:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;true&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: HTTPRoute\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-web\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  parentRefs:\n    - name: public\n      namespace: infra\n  hostnames: [&quot;shop.example.com&quot;]\n  rules:\n    - matches:\n        - path:\n            type: PathPrefix\n            value: /api\n      backendRefs:\n        - name: api\n          port: 8080\n    - backendRefs:\n        - name: web\n          port: 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">HTTPRoute\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parentRefs:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">public\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">infra\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostnames:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop.example.com&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matches:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PathPrefix\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/api\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backendRefs:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backendRefs:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>GatewayClass\u003c/strong> names the implementation, the \u003cstrong>Gateway\u003c/strong> defines listeners and is owned by the platform team, and \u003cstrong>HTTPRoute\u003c/strong> objects are owned by application teams and attach to the gateway. Selectors limit which namespaces may attach.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tls-and-limits\">TLS and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Terminate TLS at the gateway with certificates managed by an automated issuer, and keep mTLS inside the mesh. Set request timeouts, maximum body sizes and rate limits at the edge. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS and restrict allowed hosts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operating\">Operating\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get gateway,httproute -A\nkubectl describe httproute shop-web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get gateway,httproute -A\nkubectl describe httproute shop-web -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read the route \u003cstrong>status conditions\u003c/strong>: they say whether it was accepted and which parent it attached to. Roll out route changes with review, and test a canary host before moving real traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Ingress gateways and the Gateway API fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">TLS and limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Operating\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Resources to TLS and limits; 2, TLS and limits to Operating.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The GatewayClass names the implementation, the Gateway defines listeners and is owned by the platform team, and HTTPRoute objects are owned by application teams and...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One shared route object edited by many teams.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>TLS and limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Terminate TLS at the gateway with certificates managed by an automated issuer, and keep mTLS inside the mesh.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeouts or limits at the edge.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Operating \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Read the route status conditions: they say whether it was accepted and which parent it attached to.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not reading route status conditions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-route-hid-another\">Incident: One route hid another\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new route with an overlapping prefix took traffic from the checkout service.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HTTPRoute shop-web: Accepted=True\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/api          -&gt; api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/api/checkout -&gt; checkout-v2 (backend has no such path)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout errors: 3% returned 404\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A team added a more specific rule that pointed to a backend with a different path layout. Because the longest prefix wins, checkout traffic moved to it. The team added route tests in CI, reviews by the owning team and a canary hostname for new routes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One shared route object edited by many teams\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reading route status conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Terminating TLS without automatic renewal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeouts or limits at the edge\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"resources","text":"Resources"},{"id":"tls-and-limits","text":"TLS and limits"},{"id":"operating","text":"Operating"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-one-route-hid-another","text":"Incident: One route hid another"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes Gateway API","u":"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"},{"t":"Istio ingress gateways","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/traffic-management/ingress/"}],"text":"Traffic enters a cluster through a gateway before it meets the mesh. The older Ingress resource was limited, and the Gateway API replaces it with roles, richer routing and a standard that many implementations share. What you will learn The roles of GatewayClass, Gateway and routes Routing by host, path and header TLS termination and certificate handling Timeouts, limits and safe change control Resources apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Gateway metadata: name: public namespace: infra spec: gatewayClassName: istio listeners: - name: https protocol: HTTPS port: 443 hostname: shop.example.com tls: mode: Terminate certificateRefs: - name: shop-cert allowedRoutes: namespaces: from: Selector selector: matchLabels: expose: \"true\" apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: HTTPRoute metadata: name: shop-web namespace: shop spec: parentRefs: - name: public namespace: infra hostnames: [ \"shop.example.com\" ] rules: - matches: - path: type: PathPrefix value: /api backendRefs: - name: api port: 8080 - backendRefs: - name: web port: 80 The GatewayClass names the implementation, the Gateway defines listeners and is owned by the platform team, and HTTPRoute objects are owned by application teams and attach to the gateway. Selectors limit which namespaces may attach. TLS and limits Terminate TLS at the gateway with certificates managed by an automated issuer, and keep mTLS inside the mesh. Set request timeouts, maximum body sizes and rate limits at the edge. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS and restrict allowed hosts. Operating kubectl get gateway,httproute -A kubectl describe httproute shop-web -n shop Read the route status conditions : they say whether it was accepted and which parent it attached to. Roll out route changes with review, and test a canary host before moving real traffic. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Ingress gateways and the Gateway API fits together Flow in order: 1, Resources to TLS and limits; 2, TLS and limits to Operating. Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Resources Step What it does: The GatewayClass names the implementation, the Gateway defines listeners and is owned by the platform team, and HTTPRoute objects are owned by application teams and... If it fails or is weak: One shared route object edited by many teams. TLS and limits Security What it does: Terminate TLS at the gateway with certificates managed by an automated issuer, and keep mTLS inside the mesh. If it fails or is weak: No timeouts or limits at the edge. Operating Step What it does: Read the route status conditions: they say whether it was accepted and which parent it attached to. If it fails or is weak: Not reading route status conditions. Incident: One route hid another Incident A new route with an overlapping prefix took traffic from the checkout service. HTTPRoute shop-web: Accepted=True /api -> api /api/checkout -> checkout-v2 (backend has no such path) checkout errors: 3% returned 404 A team added a more specific rule that pointed to a backend with a different path layout. Because the longest prefix wins, checkout traffic moved to it. The team added route tests in CI, reviews by the owning team and a canary hostname for new routes. Common mistakes One shared route object edited by many teams Not reading route status conditions Terminating TLS without automatic renewal No timeouts or limits at the edge"},"service-mesh/traffic-shifting-retries-and-timeouts":{"html":"\u003cp>Mesh routing rules let you release gradually and add resilience without changing code. They also let you hurt yourself with a single setting, so you need to understand how weights, retries and timeouts combine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Weighted routing for canary releases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Timeouts and retries that respect idempotency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Circuit breaking and outlier detection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preventing retry storms\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"weighted-routing\">Weighted routing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: VirtualService\nmetadata:\n  name: api\nspec:\n  hosts: [api]\n  http:\n    - route:\n        - destination: {host: api, subset: v1}\n          weight: 95\n        - destination: {host: api, subset: v2}\n          weight: 5\n      timeout: 3s\n      retries:\n        attempts: 2\n        perTryTimeout: 1s\n        retryOn: connect-failure,refused-stream,503\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualService\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>}\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">95\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>}\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeout:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">3s\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retries:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">attempts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">perTryTimeout:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">1s\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retryOn:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">connect-failure,refused-stream,503\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Start the canary at a small weight, compare error rate and latency of the two subsets, and increase in steps. Roll back by setting the weight to zero.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-and-retries\">Timeouts and retries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Work backwards from the user&#39;s deadline. If the caller waits 3 seconds, three tries at 3 seconds each are pointless. Retry only \u003cstrong>idempotent\u003c/strong> operations or failures that happened before the request was processed, such as connection failures. Retrying a payment POST can charge twice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protecting-the-dependency\">Protecting the dependency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: DestinationRule\nmetadata:\n  name: api\nspec:\n  host: api\n  trafficPolicy:\n    connectionPool:\n      http: {http1MaxPendingRequests: 100, maxRequestsPerConnection: 50}\n    outlierDetection:\n      consecutive5xxErrors: 5\n      interval: 10s\n      baseEjectionTime: 30s\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DestinationRule\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trafficPolicy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">connectionPool:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http1MaxPendingRequests:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">100\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxRequestsPerConnection:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">outlierDetection:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">consecutive5xxErrors:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">interval:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10s\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">baseEjectionTime:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">30s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Connection limits\u003c/strong> act as circuit breakers by failing fast when a service is overloaded. \u003cstrong>Outlier detection\u003c/strong> ejects unhealthy endpoints for a time. Together with a \u003cstrong>retry budget\u003c/strong>, they stop callers from piling more load onto a failing service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Traffic shifting, retries and timeouts fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Weighted routing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Timeouts and retries\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Protecting the dependency\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Weighted routing to Timeouts and retries; 2, Timeouts and retries to Protecting the dependency.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Weighted routing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start the canary at a small weight, compare error rate and latency of the two subsets, and increase in steps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Per-try timeouts longer than the overall timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Timeouts and retries \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Work backwards from the user's deadline.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying non-idempotent requests.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Protecting the dependency \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Connection limits act as circuit breakers by failing fast when a service is overloaded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-retries-tripled-the-load\">Incident: Retries tripled the load\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small slowdown became an outage because every layer retried.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">frontend -&gt; api       attempts: 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api -&gt; db-proxy       attempts: 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db-proxy requests per second: 9x normal\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each layer added its own retries. A brief slowdown at the database was multiplied by nine, which kept it overloaded. The team kept retries at one layer, added timeouts that fit inside the caller&#39;s deadline and set circuit breakers so overloaded services reject quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Retrying at every layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying non-idempotent requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Per-try timeouts longer than the overall timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Moving canary weights without watching per-version errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"weighted-routing","text":"Weighted routing"},{"id":"timeouts-and-retries","text":"Timeouts and retries"},{"id":"protecting-the-dependency","text":"Protecting the dependency"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-retries-tripled-the-load","text":"Incident: Retries tripled the load"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio traffic management","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/traffic-management/"},{"t":"Envoy circuit breaking","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/upstream/circuit_breaking"}],"text":"Mesh routing rules let you release gradually and add resilience without changing code. They also let you hurt yourself with a single setting, so you need to understand how weights, retries and timeouts combine. What you will learn Weighted routing for canary releases Timeouts and retries that respect idempotency Circuit breaking and outlier detection Preventing retry storms Weighted routing apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: VirtualService metadata: name: api spec: hosts: [ api ] http: - route: - destination: { host: api , subset: v1 } weight: 95 - destination: { host: api , subset: v2 } weight: 5 timeout: 3s retries: attempts: 2 perTryTimeout: 1s retryOn: connect-failure,refused-stream,503 Start the canary at a small weight, compare error rate and latency of the two subsets, and increase in steps. Roll back by setting the weight to zero. Timeouts and retries Work backwards from the user&#39;s deadline. If the caller waits 3 seconds, three tries at 3 seconds each are pointless. Retry only idempotent operations or failures that happened before the request was processed, such as connection failures. Retrying a payment POST can charge twice. Protecting the dependency apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: DestinationRule metadata: name: api spec: host: api trafficPolicy: connectionPool: http: { http1MaxPendingRequests: 100 , maxRequestsPerConnection: 50 } outlierDetection: consecutive5xxErrors: 5 interval: 10s baseEjectionTime: 30s Connection limits act as circuit breakers by failing fast when a service is overloaded. Outlier detection ejects unhealthy endpoints for a time. Together with a retry budget , they stop callers from piling more load onto a failing service. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Traffic shifting, retries and timeouts fits together Flow in order: 1, Weighted routing to Timeouts and retries; 2, Timeouts and retries to Protecting the dependency. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Weighted routing Step What it does: Start the canary at a small weight, compare error rate and latency of the two subsets, and increase in steps. If it fails or is weak: Per-try timeouts longer than the overall timeout. Timeouts and retries Step What it does: Work backwards from the user's deadline. If it fails or is weak: Retrying non-idempotent requests. Protecting the dependency Step What it does: Connection limits act as circuit breakers by failing fast when a service is overloaded. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: Retries tripled the load Incident A small slowdown became an outage because every layer retried. frontend -> api attempts: 3 api -> db-proxy attempts: 3 db-proxy requests per second: 9x normal Each layer added its own retries. A brief slowdown at the database was multiplied by nine, which kept it overloaded. The team kept retries at one layer, added timeouts that fit inside the caller&#39;s deadline and set circuit breakers so overloaded services reject quickly. Common mistakes Retrying at every layer Retrying non-idempotent requests Per-try timeouts longer than the overall timeout Moving canary weights without watching per-version errors"},"service-mesh/mesh-authorization-and-zero-trust-policy":{"html":"\u003cp>Encryption proves who is on the other end. Authorization decides what they may do. A mesh gives you both, replacing network location rules with identities that follow the workload.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How mesh identities are issued and verified\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Strict mTLS and migration from permissive mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing allow policies and default deny\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rolling out policy without breaking traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-and-mtls\">Identity and mTLS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each workload gets a certificate tied to its Kubernetes \u003cstrong>service account\u003c/strong>, for example \u003ccode>spiffe://cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout\u003c/code>. Start with \u003cstrong>permissive\u003c/strong> mode, which accepts both plain and mTLS traffic, then move to \u003cstrong>strict\u003c/strong> after you confirm every client uses the mesh.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: PeerAuthentication\nmetadata:\n  name: default\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  mtls:\n    mode: STRICT\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">security.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PeerAuthentication\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mtls:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">STRICT\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"allow-policies\">Allow policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: AuthorizationPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-allow-checkout\n  namespace: payments\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments\n  action: ALLOW\n  rules:\n    - from:\n        - source:\n            principals: [&quot;cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout&quot;]\n      to:\n        - operation:\n            methods: [&quot;POST&quot;]\n            paths: [&quot;/charge&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">security.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AuthorizationPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments-allow-checkout\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">action:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ALLOW\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">principals:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout&quot;\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">operation:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">methods:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;POST&quot;\u003c/span>]\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/charge&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Once any ALLOW policy applies to a workload, requests that match no rule are denied. Add a namespace-wide default deny and allow each needed path explicitly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollout\">Rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inventory real traffic from mesh metrics first. Apply policies in audit or dry-run mode if your mesh supports it, watch denials in access logs and fix legitimate callers, then enforce. Keep policies in Git with owners and review, and alert on a sudden rise in denied requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get authorizationpolicy -A\nkubectl logs -n payments deploy/payments -c istio-proxy | grep RBAC\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get authorizationpolicy -A\nkubectl logs -n payments deploy/payments -c istio-proxy | grep RBAC\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Mesh authorization and zero trust policy fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Identity and mTLS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allow policies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Identity and mTLS to Allow policies; 2, Allow policies to Rollout.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Identity and mTLS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each workload gets a certificate tied to its Kubernetes service account, for example spiffe://cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Switching to strict mTLS without finding non-mesh callers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Allow policies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Once any ALLOW policy applies to a workload, requests that match no rule are denied.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Allow rules with wildcards for everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Rollout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inventory real traffic from mesh metrics first.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policies nobody owns or reviews.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-strict-mode-broke-the-batch-job\">Incident: Strict mode broke the batch job\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Enabling strict mTLS stopped a job that sent plain traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">upstream connect error or disconnect/reset before headers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">reset reason: connection termination\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">caller: batch-export (no sidecar)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The nightly export ran outside the mesh and sent plain traffic. When the namespace switched to strict, its requests were rejected. The team now inventories callers from mesh metrics before enforcing, adds exceptions with a deadline for legacy clients and tests policy changes in staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Switching to strict mTLS without finding non-mesh callers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allow rules with wildcards for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No default deny\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policies nobody owns or reviews\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"identity-and-mtls","text":"Identity and mTLS"},{"id":"allow-policies","text":"Allow policies"},{"id":"rollout","text":"Rollout"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-strict-mode-broke-the-batch-job","text":"Incident: Strict mode broke the batch job"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio security","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"Linkerd authorization policy","u":"https://linkerd.io/2/features/server-policy/"}],"text":"Encryption proves who is on the other end. Authorization decides what they may do. A mesh gives you both, replacing network location rules with identities that follow the workload. What you will learn How mesh identities are issued and verified Strict mTLS and migration from permissive mode Writing allow policies and default deny Rolling out policy without breaking traffic Identity and mTLS Each workload gets a certificate tied to its Kubernetes service account , for example spiffe://cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout . Start with permissive mode, which accepts both plain and mTLS traffic, then move to strict after you confirm every client uses the mesh. apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1 kind: PeerAuthentication metadata: name: default namespace: shop spec: mtls: mode: STRICT Allow policies apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1 kind: AuthorizationPolicy metadata: name: payments-allow-checkout namespace: payments spec: selector: matchLabels: app: payments action: ALLOW rules: - from: - source: principals: [ \"cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout\" ] to: - operation: methods: [ \"POST\" ] paths: [ \"/charge\" ] Once any ALLOW policy applies to a workload, requests that match no rule are denied. Add a namespace-wide default deny and allow each needed path explicitly. Rollout Inventory real traffic from mesh metrics first. Apply policies in audit or dry-run mode if your mesh supports it, watch denials in access logs and fix legitimate callers, then enforce. Keep policies in Git with owners and review, and alert on a sudden rise in denied requests. kubectl get authorizationpolicy -A kubectl logs -n payments deploy/payments -c istio-proxy | grep RBAC How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Mesh authorization and zero trust policy fits together Flow in order: 1, Identity and mTLS to Allow policies; 2, Allow policies to Rollout. Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Identity and mTLS Security What it does: Each workload gets a certificate tied to its Kubernetes service account, for example spiffe://cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout. If it fails or is weak: Switching to strict mTLS without finding non-mesh callers. Allow policies Security What it does: Once any ALLOW policy applies to a workload, requests that match no rule are denied. If it fails or is weak: Allow rules with wildcards for everything. Rollout Step What it does: Inventory real traffic from mesh metrics first. If it fails or is weak: Policies nobody owns or reviews. Incident: Strict mode broke the batch job Incident Enabling strict mTLS stopped a job that sent plain traffic. upstream connect error or disconnect/reset before headers reset reason: connection termination caller: batch-export (no sidecar) The nightly export ran outside the mesh and sent plain traffic. When the namespace switched to strict, its requests were rejected. The team now inventories callers from mesh metrics before enforcing, adds exceptions with a deadline for legacy clients and tests policy changes in staging. Common mistakes Switching to strict mTLS without finding non-mesh callers Allow rules with wildcards for everything No default deny Policies nobody owns or reviews"},"service-mesh/multi-cluster-mesh-and-failover":{"html":"\u003cp>Running more than one cluster gives you isolation and regional failover, but services still need to find and trust each other. A mesh can provide that, at the cost of more moving parts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Shared trust and identity across clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Discovery and gateway patterns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locality-aware routing and failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure domains and capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trust\">Trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Workloads in both clusters must trust the same \u003cstrong>root certificate authority\u003c/strong>, usually with a separate intermediate per cluster. Protect the root offline and rotate intermediates on a schedule. Without shared trust, mTLS between clusters fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"connectivity-and-discovery\">Connectivity and discovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>There are two main shapes. In \u003cstrong>flat\u003c/strong> networking the pod networks are routable, so proxies connect directly. In \u003cstrong>gateway-based\u003c/strong> designs each cluster exposes an east-west gateway and traffic crosses it over mTLS. Each control plane or a replicated service registry learns about services in the other cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl remote-clusters\nkubectl --context east get svc -n shop\nkubectl --context west get endpoints api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">istioctl remote-clusters\nkubectl --context east get svc -n shop\nkubectl --context west get endpoints api -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locality-and-failover\">Locality and failover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trafficPolicy:\n  loadBalancer:\n    localityLbSetting:\n      enabled: true\n      failover:\n        - from: eu-west\n          to: eu-central\n  outlierDetection:\n    consecutive5xxErrors: 5\n    interval: 10s\n    baseEjectionTime: 30s\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trafficPolicy:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">loadBalancer:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">localityLbSetting:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failover:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eu-west\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eu-central\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">outlierDetection:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">consecutive5xxErrors:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">interval:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10s\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">baseEjectionTime:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">30s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Prefer local endpoints for latency and cost, and fail over only when local ones are unhealthy. Outlier detection is required for failover to trigger.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"independence\">Independence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give each cluster its own control plane if possible, and keep the system able to run if the link between them is cut. Plan spare capacity so one cluster can absorb the other&#39;s traffic, and run regular failover tests during working hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Multi-cluster mesh and failover fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trust\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Connectivity and discovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Locality and failover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Independence\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Trust to Connectivity and discovery; 2, Connectivity and discovery to Locality and failover; 3, Locality and failover to Independence.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Trust \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Workloads in both clusters must trust the same root certificate authority, usually with a separate intermediate per cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming failover works without testing it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Connectivity and discovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> There are two main shapes. In flat networking the pod networks are routable, so proxies connect directly.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Sizing each cluster only for its own traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Locality and failover \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prefer local endpoints for latency and cost, and fail over only when local ones are unhealthy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Locality and failover Prefer local endpoints for latency and cost, and fail over only when local ones are unhealthy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Independence \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Give each cluster its own control plane if possible, and keep the system able to run if the link between them is cut.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cross-region traffic on every call.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-failover-overloaded-the-survivor\">Incident: Failover overloaded the survivor\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Traffic moved to the second cluster and overloaded it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eu-west: all endpoints unhealthy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eu-central: cpu 97%, p99 latency 4.2s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">503 rate rising\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The mesh failed over exactly as configured, but the second cluster was sized for half the traffic. The team sized each region to carry the other at peak, added autoscaling headroom and scheduled a quarterly failover test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming failover works without testing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sizing each cluster only for its own traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing one point of failure such as a single root key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cross-region traffic on every call\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"trust","text":"Trust"},{"id":"connectivity-and-discovery","text":"Connectivity and discovery"},{"id":"locality-and-failover","text":"Locality and failover"},{"id":"independence","text":"Independence"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-failover-overloaded-the-survivor","text":"Incident: Failover overloaded the survivor"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio multicluster","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/setup/install/multicluster/"},{"t":"Linkerd multicluster","u":"https://linkerd.io/2/features/multicluster/"}],"text":"Running more than one cluster gives you isolation and regional failover, but services still need to find and trust each other. A mesh can provide that, at the cost of more moving parts. What you will learn Shared trust and identity across clusters Discovery and gateway patterns Locality-aware routing and failover Failure domains and capacity Trust Workloads in both clusters must trust the same root certificate authority , usually with a separate intermediate per cluster. Protect the root offline and rotate intermediates on a schedule. Without shared trust, mTLS between clusters fails. Connectivity and discovery There are two main shapes. In flat networking the pod networks are routable, so proxies connect directly. In gateway-based designs each cluster exposes an east-west gateway and traffic crosses it over mTLS. Each control plane or a replicated service registry learns about services in the other cluster. istioctl remote-clusters kubectl --context east get svc -n shop kubectl --context west get endpoints api -n shop Locality and failover trafficPolicy: loadBalancer: localityLbSetting: enabled: true failover: - from: eu-west to: eu-central outlierDetection: consecutive5xxErrors: 5 interval: 10s baseEjectionTime: 30s Prefer local endpoints for latency and cost, and fail over only when local ones are unhealthy. Outlier detection is required for failover to trigger. Independence Give each cluster its own control plane if possible, and keep the system able to run if the link between them is cut. Plan spare capacity so one cluster can absorb the other&#39;s traffic, and run regular failover tests during working hours. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Multi-cluster mesh and failover fits together Flow in order: 1, Trust to Connectivity and discovery; 2, Connectivity and discovery to Locality and failover; 3, Locality and failover to Independence. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Trust Step What it does: Workloads in both clusters must trust the same root certificate authority, usually with a separate intermediate per cluster. If it fails or is weak: Assuming failover works without testing it. Connectivity and discovery Step What it does: There are two main shapes. In flat networking the pod networks are routable, so proxies connect directly. If it fails or is weak: Sizing each cluster only for its own traffic. Locality and failover Step What it does: Prefer local endpoints for latency and cost, and fail over only when local ones are unhealthy. If it fails or is weak: Locality and failover Prefer local endpoints for latency and cost, and fail over only when local ones are unhealthy. Independence Step What it does: Give each cluster its own control plane if possible, and keep the system able to run if the link between them is cut. If it fails or is weak: Cross-region traffic on every call. Incident: Failover overloaded the survivor Incident Traffic moved to the second cluster and overloaded it. eu-west: all endpoints unhealthy eu-central: cpu 97%, p99 latency 4.2s 503 rate rising The mesh failed over exactly as configured, but the second cluster was sized for half the traffic. The team sized each region to carry the other at peak, added autoscaling headroom and scheduled a quarterly failover test. Common mistakes Assuming failover works without testing it Sizing each cluster only for its own traffic Sharing one point of failure such as a single root key Cross-region traffic on every call"},"service-mesh/mesh-performance-cost-and-resource-tuning":{"html":"\u003cp>A mesh is software in the request path, so it needs capacity planning like any other service. Most surprises come from proxies holding too much configuration or from too many metric labels.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Measuring added latency and resource use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scoping proxy configuration to what a workload needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reducing telemetry cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sizing the control plane and proxies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure\">Measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the same load test against a service with and without the mesh, and compare p50 and p99 latency, CPU and memory. Typical sidecar overhead is small per request, but it adds up across many hops and many pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top pod -n shop --containers\nistioctl proxy-status\nistioctl proxy-config clusters deploy/web -n shop | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl top pod -n shop --containers\nistioctl proxy-status\nistioctl proxy-config clusters deploy/web -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The last line counts how many clusters, meaning upstream services, the proxy holds. A large number means high memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scope-configuration\">Scope configuration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: Sidecar\nmetadata:\n  name: default\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  egress:\n    - hosts:\n        - &quot;./*&quot;\n        - &quot;platform/*&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Sidecar\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">egress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;./*&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform/*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>By default each proxy learns about every service in the mesh. A \u003cstrong>Sidecar\u003c/strong> resource limits it to the namespaces a workload really calls, which saves memory and speeds configuration pushes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"telemetry\">Telemetry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>High-cardinality labels, such as user IDs or full paths, can explode metric series. Remove unneeded dimensions, sample traces and access logs, and keep detailed telemetry for important services only.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sizing\">Sizing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set requests and limits on proxies based on measurements, and size the control plane for the number of proxies and changes. Watch control plane push latency and rejected configuration, since slow pushes mean stale routing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Mesh performance, cost and resource tuning fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scope configuration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Telemetry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sizing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Measure to Scope configuration; 2, Scope configuration to Telemetry; 3, Telemetry to Sizing.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Measure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run the same load test against a service with and without the mesh, and compare p50 and p99 latency, CPU and memory.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never benchmarking with and without the mesh.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Scope configuration \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> By default each proxy learns about every service in the mesh.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving every proxy with global configuration.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Telemetry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> High-cardinality labels, such as user IDs or full paths, can explode metric series.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keeping all metric labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Sizing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Set requests and limits on proxies based on measurements, and size the control plane for the number of proxies and changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Proxy limits copied from examples.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-gigabytes-of-proxy-memory\">Incident: Gigabytes of proxy memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each proxy held configuration for every service in a large cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">istio-proxy memory: 1.4Gi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">clusters in config: 6900\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pods OOMKilled in the last hour: 31\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Nobody had scoped proxy configuration, so each sidecar tracked thousands of services. Proxies hit memory limits and restarted. A default Sidecar resource per namespace reduced configuration and memory sharply, and the team added a dashboard of proxy memory and config size.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never benchmarking with and without the mesh\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving every proxy with global configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping all metric labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Proxy limits copied from examples\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure"},{"id":"scope-configuration","text":"Scope configuration"},{"id":"telemetry","text":"Telemetry"},{"id":"sizing","text":"Sizing"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-gigabytes-of-proxy-memory","text":"Incident: Gigabytes of proxy memory"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio performance and scalability","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ops/deployment/performance-and-scalability/"},{"t":"Envoy documentation","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/"}],"text":"A mesh is software in the request path, so it needs capacity planning like any other service. Most surprises come from proxies holding too much configuration or from too many metric labels. What you will learn Measuring added latency and resource use Scoping proxy configuration to what a workload needs Reducing telemetry cost Sizing the control plane and proxies Measure Run the same load test against a service with and without the mesh, and compare p50 and p99 latency, CPU and memory. Typical sidecar overhead is small per request, but it adds up across many hops and many pods. kubectl top pod -n shop --containers istioctl proxy-status istioctl proxy-config clusters deploy/web -n shop | wc -l The last line counts how many clusters, meaning upstream services, the proxy holds. A large number means high memory. Scope configuration apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: Sidecar metadata: name: default namespace: shop spec: egress: - hosts: - \"./*\" - \"platform/*\" By default each proxy learns about every service in the mesh. A Sidecar resource limits it to the namespaces a workload really calls, which saves memory and speeds configuration pushes. Telemetry High-cardinality labels, such as user IDs or full paths, can explode metric series. Remove unneeded dimensions, sample traces and access logs, and keep detailed telemetry for important services only. Sizing Set requests and limits on proxies based on measurements, and size the control plane for the number of proxies and changes. Watch control plane push latency and rejected configuration, since slow pushes mean stale routing. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Mesh performance, cost and resource tuning fits together Flow in order: 1, Measure to Scope configuration; 2, Scope configuration to Telemetry; 3, Telemetry to Sizing. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Measure Step What it does: Run the same load test against a service with and without the mesh, and compare p50 and p99 latency, CPU and memory. If it fails or is weak: Never benchmarking with and without the mesh. Scope configuration Step What it does: By default each proxy learns about every service in the mesh. If it fails or is weak: Leaving every proxy with global configuration. Telemetry Step What it does: High-cardinality labels, such as user IDs or full paths, can explode metric series. If it fails or is weak: Keeping all metric labels. Sizing Step What it does: Set requests and limits on proxies based on measurements, and size the control plane for the number of proxies and changes. If it fails or is weak: Proxy limits copied from examples. Incident: Gigabytes of proxy memory Incident Each proxy held configuration for every service in a large cluster. istio-proxy memory: 1.4Gi clusters in config: 6900 pods OOMKilled in the last hour: 31 Nobody had scoped proxy configuration, so each sidecar tracked thousands of services. Proxies hit memory limits and restarted. A default Sidecar resource per namespace reduced configuration and memory sharply, and the team added a dashboard of proxy memory and config size. Common mistakes Never benchmarking with and without the mesh Leaving every proxy with global configuration Keeping all metric labels Proxy limits copied from examples"},"service-mesh/operating-and-upgrading-a-service-mesh":{"html":"\u003cp>The mesh sits in front of every request, so a bad upgrade can look like an outage everywhere. Staged, reversible upgrades and good health signals make it a routine operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Version skew between control plane and proxies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary upgrades with revisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rolling proxies in waves and rolling back\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to monitor during an upgrade\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revisions\">Revisions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl install --set revision=1-24 -y\nkubectl get pods -n istio-system -l app=istiod\nkubectl label namespace shop istio.io/rev=1-24 --overwrite\nkubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop\nistioctl proxy-status\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">istioctl install --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> revision=1-24 -y\nkubectl get pods -n istio-system -l app=istiod\nkubectl label namespace shop istio.io/rev=1-24 --overwrite\nkubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop\nistioctl proxy-status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003cstrong>revisions\u003c/strong> you install a second control plane beside the first. Each namespace points at one revision, so you move a test namespace first, then others. Proxies pick up the new version when their pods restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"skew-and-order\">Skew and order\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Control plane versions can usually be at most one minor version ahead of proxies. Upgrade the control plane first, then proxies, then gateways. Read release notes for removed APIs and changed defaults. Keep infrastructure code in Git so you can review the diff.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollback\">Rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rolling back is relabeling the namespace to the old revision and restarting the workloads. Do not delete the old control plane until the new one has run healthy for a full business cycle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monitor\">Monitor\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Request success rate and p99 latency by service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control plane config push errors and rejected configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Proxy and control plane restarts and memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Certificate issuance and expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Gateway error rates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Define a go or no-go check for each wave, and schedule upgrades outside peak windows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Operating and upgrading a service mesh fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Revisions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Skew and order\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Monitor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Revisions to Skew and order; 2, Skew and order to Rollback; 3, Rollback to Monitor.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Revisions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> With revisions you install a second control plane beside the first.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not restarting workloads after changing revisions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Skew and order \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Control plane versions can usually be at most one minor version ahead of proxies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping minor versions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rolling back is relabeling the namespace to the old revision and restarting the workloads.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Upgrading in place without a rollback path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Monitor \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Request success rate and p99 latency by service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Upgrading during peak traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-proxies-that-could-not-read-the-new-config\">Incident: Proxies that could not read the new config\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An in-place upgrade left old proxies with unsupported configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">istiod: pushing config to 812 proxies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">proxy: unknown field in Listener\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">412 proxies rejected configuration (NACK)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The control plane was upgraded in place two versions at once, which broke compatibility. The team rolled back, moved to one-minor-version upgrades with revisions, and added a pre-upgrade check and a canary namespace to every plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping minor versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading in place without a rollback path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not restarting workloads after changing revisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading during peak traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"revisions","text":"Revisions"},{"id":"skew-and-order","text":"Skew and order"},{"id":"rollback","text":"Rollback"},{"id":"monitor","text":"Monitor"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-proxies-that-could-not-read-the-new-config","text":"Incident: Proxies that could not read the new config"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio upgrade guide","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/setup/upgrade/"},{"t":"Linkerd upgrade guide","u":"https://linkerd.io/2/tasks/upgrade/"}],"text":"The mesh sits in front of every request, so a bad upgrade can look like an outage everywhere. Staged, reversible upgrades and good health signals make it a routine operation. What you will learn Version skew between control plane and proxies Canary upgrades with revisions Rolling proxies in waves and rolling back What to monitor during an upgrade Revisions istioctl install -- set revision=1-24 -y kubectl get pods -n istio-system -l app=istiod kubectl label namespace shop istio.io/rev=1-24 --overwrite kubectl rollout restart deployment -n shop istioctl proxy-status With revisions you install a second control plane beside the first. Each namespace points at one revision, so you move a test namespace first, then others. Proxies pick up the new version when their pods restart. Skew and order Control plane versions can usually be at most one minor version ahead of proxies. Upgrade the control plane first, then proxies, then gateways. Read release notes for removed APIs and changed defaults. Keep infrastructure code in Git so you can review the diff. Rollback Rolling back is relabeling the namespace to the old revision and restarting the workloads. Do not delete the old control plane until the new one has run healthy for a full business cycle. Monitor Request success rate and p99 latency by service Control plane config push errors and rejected configuration Proxy and control plane restarts and memory Certificate issuance and expiry Gateway error rates Define a go or no-go check for each wave, and schedule upgrades outside peak windows. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Operating and upgrading a service mesh fits together Flow in order: 1, Revisions to Skew and order; 2, Skew and order to Rollback; 3, Rollback to Monitor. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Revisions Step What it does: With revisions you install a second control plane beside the first. If it fails or is weak: Not restarting workloads after changing revisions. Skew and order Step What it does: Control plane versions can usually be at most one minor version ahead of proxies. If it fails or is weak: Skipping minor versions. Rollback Step What it does: Rolling back is relabeling the namespace to the old revision and restarting the workloads. If it fails or is weak: Upgrading in place without a rollback path. Monitor Observability What it does: Request success rate and p99 latency by service. If it fails or is weak: Upgrading during peak traffic. Incident: Proxies that could not read the new config Incident An in-place upgrade left old proxies with unsupported configuration. istiod: pushing config to 812 proxies proxy: unknown field in Listener 412 proxies rejected configuration (NACK) The control plane was upgraded in place two versions at once, which broke compatibility. The team rolled back, moved to one-minor-version upgrades with revisions, and added a pre-upgrade check and a canary namespace to every plan. Common mistakes Skipping minor versions Upgrading in place without a rollback path Not restarting workloads after changing revisions Upgrading during peak traffic"}});